  "scenario": "IP Overlay GRE Tunnel Routing",
  "goals": "• {{router2_name}} is a transit provider router. {{router1_name}} and {{router3_name}} are two customer sites that only reach each other through it, and each has a default route towards {{router2_name}}:\n    {{router1_name}}: public address {{link1_ip1}} on {{link1_cidr}}, private network {{router1_loopback1_cidr}} on Loopback1\n    {{router3_name}}: public address {{link2_ip2}} on {{link2_cidr}}, private network {{router3_loopback1_cidr}} on Loopback1\n• {{router2_name}} only knows its two directly connected public subnets and must never learn the private networks.\n• The two private networks cannot be reached today, because advertising them to {{router2_name}} is not allowed.\n• Build a GRE tunnel Tunnel{{tunnel_id}} directly between {{router1_name}} and {{router3_name}} across the transit: source it from the local public interface, send it to the remote public address, and address the tunnel itself out of {{tunnel_cidr}} ({{tunnel_ip1}} on {{router1_name}}, {{tunnel_ip2}} on {{router3_name}}).\n• Run OSPF process 1 area 0 over the tunnel only, and advertise the tunnel subnet and the local private network into it, so that each site learns the other private network through the tunnel.\n• Do not enable OSPF on the public interfaces and do not configure anything on {{router2_name}}: it must still have no route to either private network.\n• Result: the OSPF adjacency must be up over the tunnel, each site must reach the remote private network with the remote tunnel address as next hop, and {{router1_loopback1_ip}} must be able to ping {{router3_loopback1_ip}}.",
  "topology_model": {
    "nodes": [
      "r1",
      "r2",
      "r3"
    ],
    "links": [
      {
        "id": "link1",
        "nodes": [
          "r1",
          "r2"
        ],
        "media": "{{link1_media}}"
      },
      {
        "id": "link2",
        "nodes": [
          "r2",
          "r3"
        ],
        "media": "{{link2_media}}"
      }
    ]
  },
